      "pageDescription": "OCCRP&rsquo;s Belarusian member center Buro Media has launched a database profiling 20 people close to Aleksandr Lukashenko, documenting their assets, business interests, connections, and privileges.",
      "evidenceText": "Buro Media Launches Database of Lukashenko’s Inner Circle | OCCRP Corruption • Belarus Buro Media Launches Database of Lukashenko’s Inner Circle OCCRP’s Belarusian member center Buro Media has launched a database profiling 20 people close to Aleksandr Lukashenko, documenting their assets, business interests, connections, and privileges. Banner: Buro Media | Buro Media’s new database profiles 20 people close to Belarusian leader Aleksandr Lukashenko, documenting their assets, business interests, connections, and privileges. Reported by Aliaksei Hulitski Buro Media November 13, 2026 | 1 min read Share Article &times; Aa Text Settings &times; Text Size Small Medium Large Line Spacing Compact Normal Spacious OCCRP’s Belarusian member center Buro Media has launched Friday a new database, “Favorites,” documenting the wealth, assets, business interests, and connections of people close to Belarusian authoritarian leader Aleksandr Lukashenko. The project currently features 20 profiles, including members of Lukashenko’s family, security officials, businessmen, and public figures. Buro plans to expand the database with additional names. Each profile provides biographical information, details of business activities, and current and historical assets held by the individuals and their associates. The database also documents personal and professional connections, sanctions, criminal proceedings, and privileges granted through ties to Lukashenko, including preferential government loans, tax exemptions, state contracts, and exclusive import and export rights. Several people featured in the database have previously appeared in OCCRP investigations. Viktor Sheiman and Alexander Zingman were linked to a gold-mining deal in Zimbabwe, while Aliaksei Aleksin, Alexander Zaytsev, and Mikalai Varabei were featured in an investigation into Belarusian business networks in Lithuania. The Karić family was also the subject of an OCCRP investigation into lucrative real estate deals in Minsk. Buro Media said the database, current as of November 2026, is intended to help journalists, researchers, diplomats, and analysts better understand the networks of power and wealth surrounding Lukashenko. November 13, 2026 Share Article: Reported by Aliaksei Hulitski Buro Media Related Articles 1 week ago INTERVIEW The Students Beat the Strongman. Now What? Five Questions on What Comes Next For Serbia Corruption Serbia 3 weeks ago Serbian Bank Tied to Outgoing Regime Collapses After Internal Vault Theft Corruption Serbia 1 month ago Interview ‘Is This Real?’: A Serbian Investigative Journalist Reflects on the Country’s Stunning Election Freedom and Repression Serbia 1 week ago INTERVIEW The Students Beat the Strongman. Now What? Five Questions on What Comes Next For Serbia Corruption Serbia 3 weeks ago Serbian Bank Tied to Outgoing Regime Collapses After Internal Vault Theft Corruption Serbia 1 month ago Interview ‘Is This Real?’: A Serbian Investigative Journalist Reflects on the Country’s Stunning Election Freedom and Repression Serbia Help us improve the website! Click below to give us input. Your feedback is very much appreciated. 👉 Help OCCRP Don't show this anymore",
