      "pageTitle": "Google’s News AI pilot expands to over 400 publications, including U.S. local news outlets",
      "pageDescription": "In December, Google launched its News AI pilot program, which licenses content from select publishers and uses it to test new AI features in Google News. On…",
      "evidenceText": "Google’s News AI pilot expands to over 400 publications, including U.S. local news outlets | Nieman Journalism Lab Get Nieman Lab in your inbox. All the latest headlines and insights from the world of digital journalism and publishing. Our daily newsletter is a must-read from newsrooms to executive suites. Leave this empty Email address Name (optional) Subscribe We will never sell your address. Prefer a weekly email? Nearly there — check your email. We have sent a confirmation link to . Click it and you are on the list. If it has not turned up in a minute or two, it may be in your spam folder. Subscribe another address Licensed news content is used for experimental AI features, including audio briefing, a customizable podcast in the Google News app. (Courtesy of Google.) Google’s News AI pilot expands to over 400 publications, including U.S. local news outlets Link blog.google ↗ Posted by Andrew Deck October 15, 2026 In December, Google launched its News AI pilot program, which licenses content from select publishers and uses it to test new AI features in Google News. On Thursday, the company announced that the program has added new partnerships, bringing the total number of publications in the pilot to more than 400 worldwide. At launch, the program partnered with international publishers like The Guardian, El País, The Times of India, and The Washington Post. More publications are now coming on board, including CNN, The Financial Times, Axios, and Forbes. RELATED ARTICLE Discover isn’t the only place Google is experimenting with AI-generated snippets Andrew Deck January 26, 2026 As part of the expansion, the program has also added local news publishers across the U.S., including The Post and Courier in Charleston, South Carolina; Oil City News in Casper, Wyoming; the Illinois- and Iowa-based newspaper chain Shaw Media ; and the nonprofit newsroom The Texas Tribune . “People’s information consumption habits are changing, and we want to make sure publishers of all sizes, local newsrooms included, can navigate these shifts,” Brianna Puccini Duff, a spokesperson for Google, said in a statement to Nieman Lab. Google declined to comment on the exact terms of the deals. However, Duff clarified that the News AI pilot does not operate on a “ pay-per-value ” model, meaning publishers are not compensated based on how much their content contributes to an AI-generated output. (The pay-per-value model is part of a parallel program that pays publishers for content surfaced in AI Overviews, AI Mode, and Gemini, according to a recent report by Digiday .) With the News AI pilot, publishers are instead paid to provide content that appears in experimental AI-powered features. That includes “story previews” — in-depth, AI-generated article summaries of recent stories that may show up on a publisher’s Google News page. Here’s a Washington Post example, with a disclaimer noting the copy is AI-generated and “may include mistakes.” An AI-generated “story preview” on the Google News page for The Washington Post Another test feature is “audio briefing,” a customizable, AI-generated podcast in the Google News app that stitches together daily headlines. Users can select the topics they’re most interested in hearing about, including “World,” “Gaming,” or “Sports” news. The feature includes citations that link to articles mentioned in the briefing. Get Nieman Lab’s Daily Digest The most important stories about journalism and the future of news, delivered to your inbox every afternoon. Subscribe Andrew Deck is a staff writer covering AI at Nieman Lab. Have tips about how AI is being used in your newsroom? You can reach Andrew via email , Bluesky , or Signal (+1 203-841-6241). October 15, 2026 See more on   AI & Journalism Tags AI AI licensing deals AI Overviews Axios CNN Financial Times generative AI Google Google News licensing licensing deals local news Oil City News parnterships Shaw Media Texas Tribune The Post and Courier The latest from Nieman Lab Seals, construction, and midterms drive traffic gains at Massachusetts nonprofit news outlets Hanaa' Tameez NPR makes fixing its app a key priority in its new strategic plan Hanaa' Tameez Traffic is declining for local newspapers. Here are some things that helped small publishers make gains. Sophie Culpepper The Nieman Lab News-About-News Quiz, November 13, 2026 Joshua Benton Indonesia’s new copyright bill would force Google and Meta to pay to aggregate news Andrew Deck The latest from Nieman Lab Traffic is declining for local newspapers. Here are some things that helped small publishers make gains. The Cornell Daily Sun saw traffic spike by almost 500% in September. Sophie Culpepper NPR makes fixing its app a key priority in its new strategic plan Hanaa' Tameez Seals, construction, and midterms drive traffic gains at Massachusetts nonprofit news outlets And at Cambridge Day, \"having full-time reporters was transformative.\" Our latest traffic rankings for nonprofit news outlets. Hanaa' Tameez The Nieman Lab News-About-News Quiz, November 13, 2026 From new majors to search traffic, newspapers to impeachment: Have you kept up with this week's media news? Joshua Benton Some local news outlets are losing physical newsrooms Hanaa' Tameez Foundation Reports Lab Storyboard Help advance the Nieman Foundation's mission “to promote and elevate the standards of journalism” by making a donation . Subscribe Bluesky Twitter Instagram Facebook Nieman Foundation for Journalism at Harvard Walter Lippmann House, One Francis Ave. Cambridge, MA 02138 (617) 495-2237 © 2026 by the President and Fellows of Harvard College Harvard University Trademark Privacy Digital Accessibility Get Nieman Lab in your inbox. All the latest headlines and insights from the world of digital journalism and publishing. Our daily newsletter is a must-read from newsrooms to executive suites. Leave this empty Email address Name (optional) Subscribe We’ll never sell your address. Prefer a w