      "id": "e6ae2ab32d56c3ed60ec772b0a31d590ca4c03b9ff020830957335b3175bbdc9",
      "title": "IDRC John G. Bene Fellowship 2026 (up to $30,000 CAD)",
      "url": "https://opportunitydesk.org/2026/10/27/idrc-john-g-bene-fellowship-2026",
      "category": "opportunities",
      "checkedAt": "2026-11-28T02:04:02.096Z",
      "httpStatus": 200,
      "finalUrl": "https://idrc-crdi.ca/en/funding/idrc-invites-applications-john-g-bene-fellowship-2026",
      "contentType": "text/html; charset=UTF-8",
      "pageTitle": "IDRC invites applications for the John G. Bene Fellowship 2026",
      "pageDescription": "The John G Bene Fellowship provides funding to eligible students at Canadian universities to cover the cost of field research conducted in the Global South.",
      "evidenceText": "IDRC invites applications for the John G. Bene Fellowship 2026 | IDRC - International Development Research Centre Skip to main content IDRC invites applications for the John G. Bene Fellowship 2026 Open Call for Applications Deadline Monday, November 30, 2026 - 23:59 ET Programs Climate-Resilient Food Systems Duration 10 weeks to 12 months Topic(s) Development Environment Food and Agriculture Natural Resources Funded by Bene Endowment fund and IDRC Budget Up to ten awards of a maximum of CA$30,000 Point of contact benefellowship@idrc.ca Type Student award Status Open Scope IDRC has helped graduate students undertake research in the field of international development since 1982. The Bene fellowship, a bequest from the estate of John G. Bene, is offered to Canadians and permanent residents of Canada pursuing master’s or doctoral studies at a Canadian university. Candidates should have an academic background that combines an interest in forests (landscape and forest management and restoration, forestry, agroforestry), environment, ecology and food systems, with a social sciences lens (gender and inclusion, Indigenous knowledge and science, biodiversity conservation, governance of nature).   This fellowship aims to facilitate practical field research on gender-responsive integrated forest, landscape and food systems restoration and management including within fire prone, degraded or disputed landscapes. Linking the broader climate-food-inclusivity nexus, it emphasizes nature-based solutions and approaches that have the potential to drive transformative change for smallholder farmers, including youth, women, and Indigenous communities in developing countries. The successful candidate will propose research that benefits less privileged people in developing countries aiming to increase the resilience of individuals and forest/agroforest communities facing difficult contexts and challenges. Note that work focused on genomics and/or genetic modification is not eligible.   Proposals that align with IDRC's REVIVE Initiative on post-fires forest and food restauration and food systems transformation are welcome.     Learn more about John G. Bene.   Eligibility This call is open to Canadians and permanent residents of Canada pursuing master’s or doctoral studies at a Canadian university. The award under this call will cover costs of field research conducted for a master’s thesis or doctoral dissertation in one or more countries of the Global South. Please consult the detailed call document for more specific eligibility requirements. More details For more information, please read the detailed call for proposals document and frequently asked questions.   Ready to apply?  To apply for this funding opportunity, you’ll need an account on SurveyMonkey Apply. If you don’t have an account yet, or need help with your existing one, please refer to our Account setup guide . Get started Share this page    ",
