      "evidenceText": "Essential Skills for New Managers (March 2027) - Poynter Essential Skills for New Managers (March 2027) - Poynter DONATE What do you want to search? Submit Sign In | Membership News Training Ethics Fact-Checking Media Literacy AI Events Future of Public Media Newmark Ethics Center IFCN MediaWise PolitiFact Donate About Us Press Freedom Watch The Poynter 50 Podcasts Artificial Intelligence Business & Work Reporting & Editing Fact-Checking Ethics & Trust Local News Journalism, Media & Communication Training Poynter Membership Categories Consulting & Coaching For ACES Members For Local News Funders All Categories Broadcast & Visual Journalism Business & Work Ethics Fact-Checking & Media Literacy Leadership Reporting & Editing Writing Poynter ACES Introductory Certificate in Editing Poynter ACES Intermediate Certificate in Editing Poynter ACES Advanced Certificate in Editing Workshops and Coaching Tools for Funders Ethics Ethics Training Ethics Articles AI Ethics Public Editor Project Get Ethics Advice Fact-Checking Articles IFCN IFCN Grants International Fact-Checking Day Teen Creator Network PolitiFact MediaWise Media Literacy Resources AI Literacy Ambassadors Teen Creator Network Research & News International en Español MediaWise Training All Events The 2026 Poynter Journalism Prizes \" * \" indicates required fields Section One: Personal Information Thank you for your interest in our Essential Skills, our Poynter Leadership Academy, our Leadership Academy for Women, or our Lead with Influence programs. There is a single application for all Poynter Leadership programs. Your information will not be saved until you submit this application. Please be ready with: your professional experience, areas of interest, and basic demographic information before starting this application. An incomplete application will be reviewed as is. ||| For all of our in-person leadership programs, we also require a reference letter. To complete this form, you must list your reference’s name, title, email and relationship to you. The reference letter itself should be emailed to info@poynter.org no later than one week past the application deadline. More details are within this application. ||| Questions? Email us at info@poynter.org. This field is hidden when viewing the form Date submitted MM slash DD slash YYYY Name * First Last Preferred name (nickname) Job title * Organization * Business email address * Personal email address * Cell phone number * Please include country code if outside the U.S. If no phone number is available, please say none. Work phone number Preferred Address * Home Work Other Street address * City * State * Select Alabama Alaska Arizona Arkansas California Colorado Connecticut Delaware District of Columbia Florida Georgia Hawaii Idaho Illinois Indiana Iowa Kansas Kentucky Louisiana Maine Maryland Massachusetts Michigan Minnesota Mississippi Missouri Montana Nebraska Nevada New Hampshire New Jersey New Mexico New York North Carolina North Dakota Ohio Oklahoma Oregon Pennsylvania Rhode Island South Carolina South Dakota Tennessee Texas Utah Vermont Virginia Washington West Virginia Wisconsin Wyoming Other Postal Code * Country (if other than United States) or U.S. territory Please note that we can accept only English-fluent candidates whose newsrooms have operating structures similar to U.S.-based newsrooms. Section Two: Professional Information Do you have a preference for which program you would like to participate in? * We will attempt to accommodate your preferred program, but it may not be guaranteed. If our selection committee thinks you are a better fit for another program, we will reach out to you directly. Lead with Influence (February 2 - March 9, virtual, weekly) Essential Skills for New Managers (March 8-12, in-person) Poynter Leadership Academy (April 12-16, in-person) Leadership Academy for Women (May 3-7, in-person) Lead with Influence (September 1 - October 6, virtual, weekly) Essential Skills for New Managers (November 8-12, in-person) Poynter can decide which is best for me Brief Bio * Please paste a brief bio (100 words or less) written in third person; be sure to include your current role and organization. (e.g., Dylan Smyth is a City Editor at the Anytown Daily, in Springfield, Illinois. Smyth was previously a Metro Reporter at the Daily and is a graduate of Springfield University.) Briefly explain your leadership role. If you train or otherwise informally manage others (including freelancers, new hires, or interns) or manage projects, please explain how you demonstrate leadership in your organization. * (Limit 100 words.) Have you applied for this program before? * Yes No If yes to the above, please describe how your role/responsibilities have changed since your previous application. * (Limit 100 words.) LinkedIn profile * Are you a manager with direct reports? * Yes No If yes, how many people do you supervise? None 1-5 6-10 More than 10 How many years of people leadership experience do you have over your entire career? * (This can include at other organizations or in other positions.) Less than 1 year 2-3 years 4-5 years 6-10 More than 10 years If more than 10 years, how long? Briefly share why you think you need leadership training at this point in your career. * Please also list any previous leadership training you’ve had. (Limit 200 words.) Tell us what leadership problems you’re trying to solve, what questions you’re hoping to answer through this training, and what skills or competencies you’d like to boost. * (Limit 200 words.) Reference Letter All applicants for our in-person leadership programs must submit a reference letter from their current supervisor or someone who can write about their career trajectory. Please ask them to email this letter to Poynter at info@poynter.org. The subject line should be \"Leadership Program Applicant: Last Name, Reference Letter” * Ask your reference to highlight your current influence as a leader and how this training might impact your leadership and career trajectory. I understand and will share this information with my supervisor. Name and title of reference * We recommend you ask someone who works closely with you who can speak to your demonstrated leadership ability as well as your potential for career growth. Reference email address * What is the relationship of the reference person to you? * Direct supervisor in current organization Direct supervisor in another organization Mentor Colleague/peer in current organization Colleague/peer in another organization College instructor Other Tuition & Stipends: These are tuition-based programs. Participants will also need to cover travel expenses. Very limited stipends may be available. Please indicate whether you or your employer can cover tuition and/or travel expenses for your participation. * Please start by asking your organization to support your professional development. Stipends will be reserved for those who show the greatest need. (Your answer to this question will not be a factor as judges review your application.) My employer will cover participation expenses, including travel. I can cover participation expenses, including travel. Neither I nor my employer is able to cover expenses. I request consideration for a stipend. If you do have a hardship case, please share more information with us here or email us at info@poynter.org. (100 words or less.) Section Three: General/Demographic Information We collect demographic data to help us measure our reach, understand the communities we serve, and report on our impact as a nonprofit organization. Providing information about your race, ethnicity, gender and pronouns is entirely voluntary. Choosing not to provide these details will have absolutely no negative impact on your application or acceptance. If you prefer not to answer, you can select \"Prefer not to respond\" or leave these questions blank. How did you hear about this program? Select all that apply. Poynter's Pro Weekly Other Poynter Email Poynter.org Co-worker or Manager Past Program Participant Other Organization Social Media: LinkedIn, Facebook, X, Instagram General Online Search Other We'd love to thank the alum who recommended you. What is their name? If other, please specify how or where. Have you participated in Poynter teaching? * (Select all that apply.) Yes, in person in Florida or at a regional workshop Yes, online (virtually) No Other If other, please specify how. Race/Ethnicity (Select all that apply.) American Indian or Alaska Native Asian Black or African American Hispanic or Latino Middle Eastern or North African Multiracial and/or Multiethnic Native Hawaiian or Pacific Islander White I prefer not to respond or self-describe I prefer to self-describe my race as Gender Male Female Non-Binary I prefer not to respond or self-describe I prefer to self-describe my gender as Pronouns He/Him She/Her They/Them He/Them She/Them I prefer not to respond or self-describe I prefer to self-describe my pronouns as Newsroom circulation/broadcast market size * Organization staff size * Check here to acknowledge that you understand and will abide by Poynter's Code of Conduct. * Code of Conduct The Poynter Institute is committed to providing a safe and welcoming environment for all. This code of conduct outlines our expectations for behavior by participants and guest instructors at Poynter-organized events as well as the consequences of unacceptable behavior. We expect all attendees (speakers, participants, staff and guests) to abide by this code of conduct at all event venues and event-related social functions. Expected Behavior First and foremost, treat everyone with respect. Participate while acknowledging that everyone deserves respectful treatment, and that every participant has the right to enjoy their experience without fear of harassment or discrimination. • Be considerate, respectful and collaborative. • Refrain from demeaning, discriminatory or harassing behavior and speech. • Immediately inform Poynter CFO/COO Jessi Navarro in person, by phone (727-337-7131) or email (jnavarro@poynter.org) if you observe or experience unwelcome and offensive behavior. • Seek permission from anyone involved in a program before quoting them or sharing materials that may have been presented. • Some Poynter events are shared with members of the public; please be respectful to all patrons and staff. Unacceptable Behavior Unacceptable behavior includes, but is not limited to, intimidating, threatening, harassing, abusive, discriminatory, derogatory, demeaning or disorderly conduct; or possession, use or distribution of any illegal substances. Poynter does not tolerate the use or abuse of illegal substances at any of the venues where Poynter events are held. Harassing conduct can take many forms. Sexually harassing conduct can be by a person of the same or opposite sex. Poynter reserves the right in its sole discretion to determine what constitutes unacceptable behavior. Consequences of Unacceptable Behavior Unacceptable behavior will not be tolerated whether by other attendees, media, speakers or venue staff. Anyone asked to stop engaging in unacceptable behavior is expected to comply immediately. If a participant engages in unacceptable behavior, Poynter may take any action it deems appropriate, up to and including immediate expulsion without warning from the event and without refund of any associated fees. What to Do if You Witness or Are Subject to Unacceptable Behavior Harassment will not be tolerated by Poynter. If you are subject to unacceptable behavior, notice that someone else is being subject to unacceptable behavior or have any other concerns, please immediately notify security staff and/or Poynter CFO/COO Jessi Navarro in person, by phone (727-337-7131) or e-mail (jnavarro@poynter.org). Also notify appropriate law enforcement if necessary. Poynter will be available to help participants contact venue security or local law enforcement, to provide escorts, or to otherwise assist those experiencing unacceptable behavior to feel safe for the duration of the event. https://www.poynter.org/poynter-institute-code-ethics/#conduct I certify I have read and understand Poynter's Code of Conduct listed above. Thank you for your application. It will be processed once you press \"submit.\" After the application deadline, we will go through a period of review. No payment is necessary at this time. We will contact you with further details if you are accepted. A copy of this application will be emailed to you as confirmation. Δ Sign In Register Sign into your account Sign In or Register to Enroll - OR - Checkout as a Guest Remember Me Reset Password Don't have an account? Register Sign In Register Register a new account Sign In or Register to Enroll Password should contain at least: Eight characters One uppercase letter One lowercase letter One number One of the following special characters: @ # - _ $ * % ^ & + = § ! ? Already have an account? Sign In Sign In Register Essential Skills for New Managers (March 2027) $ 1,550.00 Essential Skills for New Managers (March 2027) quantity Apply Now Leadership Reporting & Editing St. Petersburg, Fla. Essential Skills for New Managers (March 2027) Apply by Dec. 7, 2026, for a five-day, in-person program designed for new managers ready to build the practical skills and confidence to lead people and teams effectively. Through hands-on training, personalized feedback and peer learning, you’ll practice essential skills for delegation, coaching, difficult conversations and managing up. This program takes place March 8–12, 2027, at Poynter’s campus in St. Petersburg, Florida. March 8, 2027 – March 12, 2027 Overview Submit your application by Dec. 7, 2026. Meet March 8–12, 2027, for five days of in-person training at Poynter’s campus in St. Petersburg, Florida. Learn directly from Poynter faculty Tony Elkins and Megan Griffith-Greene. Discover and leverage your unique strengths with a Gallup CliftonStrengths report. Practice real-world management skills through hands-on exercises, peer discussions and coaching. Work through the challenges of transitioning from colleague to manager alongside peers navigating similar experiences. Get 360-degree feedback from your direct reports, supervisor and peers with Poynter’s ViewPoynt tool. Build relationships with a cohort of new managers through an immersive, transformational leadership experience. Essential Skills for New Managers (March 2027) quantity Apply Now SKU: 82704 Tags: At Poynter , Fernanda Camarena , front page course , In-person , Leadership , Tony Elkins Learning Outcomes In this interactive, five-day workshop, you will: Understand your strengths and how they shape your approach to leadership. Learn how to delegate effectively and set clear expectations. Give constructive feedback and coach employees. Gain strategies for navigating difficult workplace conversations. Learn how to onboard, develop and retain talented employees. Apply strategies for managing up and building stronger working relationships. Understand how to lead distributed teams with different work styles and needs. Develop strategies for managing your workload more sustainably. Apply by Dec 7, 2026 $ 1,550.00 Essential Skills for New Managers (March 2027) quantity Apply Now Overview Submit your application by Dec. 7, 2026. Meet March 8–12, 2027, for five days of in-person training at Poynter’s campus in St. Petersburg, Florida. Learn directly from Poynter faculty Tony Elkins and Megan Griffith-Greene. Discover and leverage your unique strengths with a Gallup CliftonStrengths report. Practice real-world management skills through hands-on exercises, peer discussions and coaching. Work through the challenges of transitioning from colleague to manager alongside peers navigating similar experiences. Get 360-degree feedback from your direct reports, supervisor and peers with Poynter’s ViewPoynt tool. Build relationships with a cohort of new managers through an immersive, transformational leadership experience. Training 5 or More People? get consulting & coaching Description Schedule Requirements Essential Skills for New Managers is for people with three or fewer years of management experience who want to build the practical skills and confidence to lead people and teams effectively. If you’re navigating the transition from colleague to manager, learning how to delegate or figuring out how to give feedback and handle difficult conversations, this program is designed for you. Apply if you’re new to managing people Over five days at Poynter’s campus in St. Petersburg, Florida, you’ll build practical skills to delegate effectively, give feedback and coaching, manage up, navigate challenging conversations and lead different types of teams. You’ll also gain a better understanding of your own strengths and leadership style through Poynter’s signature 360-degree feedback tool, the ViewPoynt, personalized small-group coaching and a Gallup CliftonStrengths assessment. You’ll put what you learn into practice through exercises, peer discussions and small-group coaching, with opportunities to work through real management challenges you’re facing. This in-person program will be offered twice in 2027: March 8–12 and Nov. 8–12.   Why learn leadership with Poynter? The Poynter Institute is a global nonprofit working to strengthen journalism to help address society’s most pressing problems. The world’s top journalists and emerging media leaders rely on the Institute to learn new skills, adopt best practices, better serve audiences, scale operations and foster trustworthy information. Poynter’s leadership training draws on more than 50 years of experience strengthening leaders in media. Our faculty bring deep expertise in management, leadership, individual coaching, self-assessment frameworks, conflict management and more. Every program emphasizes practical, hands-on learning you can put to work immediately, along with intentional cohort building that creates space for meaningful connection and shared experience. Essential Skills is part of a broader Poynter leadership track, with programs for different stages and types of leadership in media. Whether you’re leading a project without formal authority, managing people for the first time or taking on greater organizational responsibility, there’s a program designed to help you grow. The leadership track: Lead with Influence : Lead projects and influence others without formal authority; no formal management experience required, happens in February (apply by Jan. 8, 2027) and September (apply by July 9, 2027) Essential Skills for New Managers : Build the fundamentals of managing people; 0-3 years of management experience, happens in March (apply by Dec. 7, 2026) and November (apply by Aug. 16, 2027) Poynter Leadership Academy : Strengthen your skills as an experienced or senior leader; 3+ years of leadership/management experience (apply by Jan. 18, 2027) Leadership Academy for Women : Advanced leadership development centered on the experiences of women; 3+ years of leadership/management experience (apply by Feb. 8, 2027) Questions? If you need assistance, email us at info@poynter.org . This in-person-only program takes place March 8-12, 2027, in St. Petersburg, Florida, at the Poynter Institute. Sessions will take place from 9 a.m. to 5 p.m. Eastern time most days. Lunch, refreshments and light snacks are provided during the program. Accepted applicants must plan to arrive on site no later than the start date and time of Essential Skills (no late arrivals), and should expect to dedicate uninterrupted time to the program for its duration. Below is a tentative schedule, subject to change: Day 1 | Learning About Your Management Style and Focusing on Strengths Start by exploring who you are as a manager. You’ll examine your leadership style, dig into your Gallup CliftonStrengths results and identify the strengths you can leverage as you grow as a leader. D",
