      "pageTitle": "The ‘60’ Blame Game",
      "pageDescription": "The Trump administration’s attack on a “60 Minutes” report has prompted behind-the-scenes finger pointing at CBS News, where Status has learned irate staffers are accusing network brass of throwing its journalists under the bus.",
      "evidenceText": "The ‘60’ Blame Game The ‘60’ Blame Game The Trump administration’s attack on a “60 Minutes” report has prompted behind-the-scenes finger pointing at CBS News, where Status has learned irate staffers are accusing network brass of throwing its journalists under the bus. Oliver Darcy Nov 5, 2026 The ‘60’ Blame Game The Trump administration’s attack on a “60 Minutes” report has prompted behind-the-scenes finger pointing at CBS News, where Status has learned irate staffers are accusing network brass of throwing its journalists under the bus. Oliver Darcy Nov 5, 2026 Late last week, the New York Post published a story about \"60 Minutes,\" reporting that CBS News chief Bari Weiss and the newsmagazine's executive producer, Nick Bilton , were \"kept in the dark\" about a consequential editorial decision involving an immigration story that had aired on the program. The segment, reported by new contributor Gianna Toboni , examined the Trump administration's controversial practice of so-called \"third-country deportations.\" It centered, in part, on Moussa Diawara , a man deported by the U.S. to Ghana , whose government subsequently sent him to Mali —the very country a U.S. judge had ruled he should not be returned to over fears of persecution. Shortly after the segment aired, the Department of Homeland Security went ballistic on CBS News. The agency accused Diawara of committing immigration fraud, pointing to his claim on an asylum application that he was gay, despite now being married to a woman with children. That detail, however, had been omitted from the \"60 Minutes\" report, which instead attributed Diawara's fears of persecution in Mali to his conversion to Christianity. I'm told that Toboni and the \"60 Minutes\" team working on the story made a deliberate decision to exclude the claim about Diawara's sexuality, fearing that publicizing it could expose him to further persecution in Mali, where homosexuality is criminalized. Whatever the rationale, the editorial decision left \"60 Minutes\" vulnerable to criticism from DHS, and the Trump administration wasted little time exploiting it. The agency appeared to leak Diawara's asylum application to Fox News . ICE then went on the offensive on X , calling the “60 Minutes” story “Fake & Gay!\" The ensuing firestorm… The rest of this story is for paid subscribers only. Unlock the Rest Already a subscriber? Sign in . Scoop-driven reporting and sharp-edged analysis. See why The Wall Street Journal declared Status a “must-read.” Keep reading VIEW MORE About Us Archive Go Premium Quick Links Home Power Lines Archive Shop Gift About Us Advertise With Us Authors Oliver Darcy Jon Passantino Natalie Korach Brian Lowry Status is the definitive nightly briefing that cuts through the noise to inform readers about what is really happening in the corridors of media power. JOIN © 2026 Status News Inc.. Powered by beehiiv",
